e conjecture in the case where d = 3. Sheehan’s conjecture was subsequently shown to hold for all odd d ≥ 3 by Thomason [13] using a beautiful, non-constructive, parity-based argument, and for all d ≥ 300 by Thomassen [14, 16] using an ingenious combination of Thomason’s argument and the Lovász local lemma. It is worth mentioning that by a result of Petersen [11], proving Sheehan’s conjecture in the case where d = 4 would in turn imply the conjecture for all even d ≥ 4; we refer the reader to the paper of Haxell, Seamone and Verstraëte [7] for both the current state of the art as well as a discussion of why existing methods are unlikely to settle Conjecture 1.2 in its full generality.\n\n2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLine Percolation in Finite Projective Planes (https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.00531)\nciteturn4academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.3 years ago; Title: Line Percolation in Finite Projective Planes ... Additional results on the minimal and maximal percolation time as well as on the critical probability in the projective plane are also presented.\nTitle: Line Percolation in Finite Projective Planes\nAuthors: Dániel Gerbner, Balázs Keszegh, Gábor Mészáros, Balázs Patkós, Máté Vizer\nDate: Mon Aug  1 19:14:02 2016\n\nWe study combinatorial parameters of a recently introduced bootstrap percolation problem in finite projective planes. We present sharp results on the size of the minimum percolating sets and the maximal non-percolating sets. Additional results on the minimal and maximal percolation time as well as on the critical probability in the projective plane are also presented.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBULLETIN OF THE ICA (https://www.the-ica.org/Volumes/105/Reprints/BICA2024-49-Reprint.pdf)\nciteturn4search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; the only non-trivial 2-factor Hamiltonian regular bipartite graphs.Conjecture 3.2 (Funk et al. 